That is less true in Slash'em than in Nethack.  You find more items, but 
  it is nontrivial to get a safe combination.  You even have to be 
prepared to change your scale mail or to spend a wish on a cloak of 
magic resistance, which is almost as rare in Slash'em as in NH, except 
when vampires wear that cloak, which tends to make them tougher (but 
there are other ways beyond wishing to get the cloak).

>>                    So if the game becomes more deterministic and less 
>>dependent on the RNG - for characters that have already survived the 
>>first 50000 turns - that's good, not bad, IMHO.

> It makes the late game more boring. Of course, if you lose a late game
> character the frustration is immense, but that possibility can only make the
> game more exciting.

I am not advocating for winning at probability 1 after a certain stage. 
   Neither for removing instadeaths.  You should be kept awake by those 
possible failures.  I am advocating against dependence on the RNG.

In fact, despite its abundance of stuff lying around, Slash'em is much 
less boring than NH during the stage where you know you will almost 
certainly win when you don't make major mistakes any more.  The reason 
is that in NH that stage (Gehennom) is lengthy (and has to be, otherwise 
you will not find enough stuff in the mazes for a good ascension kit) 
and repetitive - lots of mazes all alike.  In Slash'em that stage is 
much shorter and there are only very few noninteresting maze levels. 
Almost all the levels of hell are special levels.  Demogorgon seems to 
be stronger than in NH and you will always have to deal with him (I 
never met him in a peaceful state).  The castle is at about level 40, so 
you will have to survive longer to get to the state where you can make 
sure with a few wishes to have all you need to ascend.  On the other 
hand, Slash'em would not miss much if there were no wands of wishing at 
all and magic lamps were made the only source of wishes (more in style 
anyway)  -- because you usually can get all your equipment without any 
wishes and by the time you have reached the castle, you will have it 
with the possible exception of one or two items.
